# Prisma Decimal Serialization Rules

## Overview

When working with Prisma Decimal fields in Next.js applications, always implement proper serialization to prevent "Decimal objects are not supported" errors when passing data from Server Components to Client Components.

## Required Global Setup

### 1. Prisma Client Configuration (src/lib/prisma.ts)

```typescript
import { PrismaClient, Prisma } from "@prisma/client";

// Global fix for Decimal serialization in Client Components
// This ensures all Decimal objects are automatically converted to numbers when serialized
if (typeof Prisma.Decimal.prototype.toJSON === "undefined") {
    // @ts-ignore - Override the toJSON method to return number instead of string
    Prisma.Decimal.prototype.toJSON = function () {
        return this.toNumber();
    };
}

// Extend the Decimal prototype to include our custom toJSON method in types
declare module "@prisma/client/runtime/library" {
    interface Decimal {
        toJSON(): number;
    }
}

declare global {
    var prismaGlobal: PrismaClient | undefined;
}

export const prisma: PrismaClient = global.prismaGlobal ?? new PrismaClient();

if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== "production") {
    global.prismaGlobal = prisma;
}
```

## Type Definition Rules

### ✅ Client-Side Types (Always Use number)

```typescript
// In src/lib/atoms.ts or type definition files
export type EstimateItem = {
    id: string;
    description: string;
    quantity: number;
    unitPrice: number; // ✅ Use number for client-side
    estimateId: string;
};

export type Service = {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    defaultPrice: number; // ✅ Use number for client-side
    // ... other fields
};
```

### ❌ Avoid These Patterns

```typescript
// ❌ Don't use Decimal in client-side types
export type EstimateItem = {
    unitPrice: Decimal; // Will cause serialization errors
};

// ❌ Don't mix types unnecessarily
export type Service = {
    defaultPrice: Decimal | number; // Prefer just number
};
```

## Server Component Serialization Rules

### ✅ Always Serialize Before Passing to Client

```typescript
// In Server Component pages (app/*/page.tsx)
export default async function EstimatesPage() {
    const data = await getEstimatesData();

    // ✅ REQUIRED: Serialize before passing to client
    const serializedData = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(data));

    return (
        <HydrationBoundary hydrateAtoms={[[atom, serializedData]]}>
            <EstimatesView />
        </HydrationBoundary>
    );
}
```

### ✅ Remove Explicit Return Types for Server Functions

```typescript
// ✅ Let TypeScript infer the return type
async function getEstimatesData() {
    const estimates = await prisma.estimate.findMany({
        include: { items: true },
    });
    return { estimates };
}

// ❌ Don't force client types on server data
async function getEstimatesData(): Promise<{ estimates: Estimate[] }> {
    // This will cause type errors because server returns Decimal
}
```

## API Route Serialization Rules

### ✅ Always Serialize API Responses

```typescript
// In API routes (app/api/*/route.ts)
export async function GET() {
    const data = await prisma.model.findMany(/* ... */);

    // ✅ REQUIRED: Serialize before returning
    const serializedData = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(data));
    return NextResponse.json(serializedData);
}

export async function POST(request: Request) {
    const newData = await prisma.model.create(/* ... */);

    // ✅ REQUIRED: Serialize before returning
    const serializedData = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(newData));
    return NextResponse.json(serializedData);
}
```

## Common Patterns and Templates

### Server Component Template

```typescript
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";
import { HydrationBoundary } from "jotai-ssr";
import { initialDataAtom } from "@/lib/atoms";
import ClientView from "@/components/views/ClientView";

// ✅ No explicit return type - let TypeScript infer
async function getData() {
    const data = await prisma.model.findMany({
        include: { relatedItems: true },
    });
    return { data };
}

export default async function Page() {
    const { data } = await getData();

    // ✅ Always serialize before passing to client
    const serializedData = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(data));

    return (
        <HydrationBoundary hydrateAtoms={[[initialDataAtom, serializedData]]}>
            <ClientView />
        </HydrationBoundary>
    );
}
```

### API Route Template

```typescript
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";

export async function GET() {
    try {
        const data = await prisma.model.findMany({
            include: { itemsWithDecimals: true },
        });

        // ✅ Always serialize responses with Decimal fields
        const serializedData = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(data));
        return NextResponse.json(serializedData);
    } catch (error) {
        return NextResponse.json(
            { error: "Internal Server Error" },
            { status: 500 }
        );
    }
}
```

## Troubleshooting Checklist

When you encounter: "Only plain objects can be passed to Client Components. Decimal objects are not supported."

1. **✅ Verify Global Setup**

    - Check that `src/lib/prisma.ts` has the toJSON override
    - Ensure the module augmentation is present

2. **✅ Check Type Definitions**

    - Client-side types use `number` not `Decimal`
    - Remove unused `Decimal` imports from client files

3. **✅ Verify Serialization**

    - Server Components serialize data before passing to client
    - API routes serialize responses before returning
    - HydrationBoundary receives serialized data

4. **✅ Remove Problematic Patterns**
    - Remove explicit return types that include `Decimal`
    - Don't use `as any` to bypass type checking

## Performance Notes

-   `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())` is acceptable for most use cases
-   The toJSON override has minimal performance impact
-   For very large datasets, consider streaming or pagination
-   Always profile if performance becomes a concern

## Testing Considerations

```typescript
// ✅ Test serialization behavior
test("serializes decimal fields correctly", () => {
    const mockData = { unitPrice: new Decimal("10.99") };
    const serialized = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(mockData));
    expect(typeof serialized.unitPrice).toBe("number");
    expect(serialized.unitPrice).toBe(10.99);
});
```

This rule set ensures consistent Decimal handling across all Next.js + Prisma projects and prevents common serialization errors.
